NAME
    g3d - the C wireframe-3D library (//#use g3d)

SYNOPSIS
    //#use gfx
    //#use g3d          in that order (g3d draws through gfx)

DESCRIPTION
    Wireframe 3D: describe a world as edges between 16-bit (x,y,z) points,
    choose a window (what you look at) and a viewport (where on screen it
    lands), and render. World axes: x right, y UP, z into the screen,
    camera at the origin looking down +z. Keep coordinates within about
    +/-16000. Black and white: the render erases its viewport to black and
    draws white lines.

    The SAME program runs three ways, fastest fitted path chosen at
    runtime: all in software; with the MDU (the hardware multiply-divide
    unit) accelerating the arithmetic; or through the GEOMETRY ENGINE,
    which holds the edge list itself and does the whole transform / clip /
    project / draw per frame in hardware, with page-flipped (tear-free)
    output.

BUILDING AND RENDERING A WORLD
    g3clear()                        empty the edge pool (512 edges max)
    g3line(x0,y0,z0, x1,y1,z1)       add one edge
    g3window(wx0,wy0,wx1,wy1)        view-plane rectangle to look at
    g3view(vx0,vy0,vx1,vy1)          screen rectangle to draw into --
                                     also a true CLIP rectangle, so
                                     viewports can sit side by side
    g3persp(d)                       focal length; 0 = orthographic
                                     (d=256: a point at z=256 maps 1:1)
    g3render()                       erase the viewport, draw the pool.
                                     Uses the engine when fitted
                                     (identical pixels), else software.

THE ENGINE PATH (when fitted -- probe with g3probe())
    A static model is uploaded ONCE; each frame is then just a matrix and
    one command, and the CPU is nearly idle:

    g3up()                upload the pool as the engine's edge list
    g3mat(m)              m = int[12]: a 3x3 matrix in 8.8 fixed point
                          (256 = 1.0), row-major, then tx,ty,tz --
                          applied to every vertex as (M*v >> 8) + T
    g3flags(f)            bit0 = erase viewport, bit1 = page-flip after
                          (power-on default 3)
    g3go()                render the uploaded list now
    g3flip()              flip pages by hand (CPU-drawn double buffering)
    g3sync()              draw page REJOINS the display page. Flips leave
                          the two pages opposite; call this before normal
                          drawing/POINT use, and clear BOTH pages once
                          before the first flip (their power-on contents
                          are undefined). See cube.c for the idiom.

    muldiv(a,b,c)         the library's arithmetic core, usable directly:
                          (a*b)/c signed via a 32-bit intermediate,
                          truncated toward zero, saturating at +/-32767.
                          Routed through the MDU when one is fitted.

EXAMPLE (spin a triangle)
    //#use gfx
    //#use g3d
    ... build pool with g3line, set window/view/persp ...
    g3up(); g3mat(m); g3go();        /* per frame: new m, g3go */

NOTES
    Sources: /lib/lib_g3d.c on this disk. The design and the measured
    numbers live in the repo: fpga/tang-nano-20k/sdram/STAGE7-DESIGN.md,
    STAGE8-DESIGN.md and STAGE8B-DESIGN.md. `cube` is the worked example.

